# Erdős Problem #99 — singleton/hole incidence coupling

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** audited-working stopped-train decomposition; strict-rhombus hole charge; detour-free exceptional-train gate  
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The existing strict-rhombus hole theorem charges every bounded-hole boundary edge to a bounded exceptional-face edge incidence:

\[
P_{\rm hole}\le 4f+Q.
\]

The exceptional-singleton gate separately counts singleton stopped trains having at least one bounded exceptional-face terminal incidence; call that number `S_E`.

These two charges are not independent. A hole-boundary edge belongs to a strict rhombus, while a singleton stopped-train edge belongs to no strict rhombus. Therefore their chosen exceptional-face incidences are disjoint. The exact sharpened budget is

\[
\boxed{P_{\rm hole}+S_E\le4f+Q.}
\tag{SH.1}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{5g_R+S_E\le4f+Q,}
\tag{SH.2}
\]

where `g_R` is the total number of bounded complementary components of the full strict-rhombus complex.

This strengthens two rhombic-complex outputs. If `T_R` is the number of non-singleton rhombic stopped trains, then

\[
\boxed{
\dim\mathcal F_{\rm train}
\ge T_R-{2\over5}(4f+Q-S_E),
}
\tag{SH.3}
\]

and if `N_oo` denotes rhombic trains whose two ends lie on the unbounded-component boundary of the strict-rhombus complex, then

\[
\boxed{
N_{oo}\ge T_R-(4f+Q)+S_E.
}
\tag{SH.4}
\]

Thus the exceptional-singleton escape simultaneously removes capacity from bounded rhombic holes and creates additional exact one-coordinate shears in the isolated rhombus subframework.

Combining (SH.1) with the detour-free gate gives a correlated lower bound containing no separate `S_E` term. Put

\[
X:=Q-f,
\qquad
T_R=2\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2},
\]

and

\[
c_{DF}:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00105
=0.167964643466509\ldots .
\]

The detour-free gate is

\[
\sigma_R+2S_E
\ge c_{DF}D+{5\over2}k+X-o(D).
\tag{SH.5}
\]

Then (SH.3) implies

\[
\boxed{
\dim\mathcal F_{\rm train}
\ge
2\sqrt{R_4}
+{c_{DF}\over5}D
+{k\over2}
-2f-{X\over5}
+{3\over10}\sigma_R
-o(D),
}
\tag{SH.6}
\]

while (SH.4) implies

\[
\boxed{
N_{oo}
\ge
2\sqrt{R_4}
+{c_{DF}\over2}D
+{5\over4}k
-5f-{X\over2}
-o(D).
}
\tag{SH.7}
\]

Equations (SH.6)--(SH.7) do not close C-II by row count: non-rhombus contact bars and diameter bars can still lock these rhombic-subframework motions. Their value is that the large-`S_E` branch can no longer be treated as simultaneously maximizing bounded-hole topology. Any later locking theorem may use the reduced hole budget rather than paying `4f+Q` and `S_E` independently.

---

## 1. Incidence partition

Let `mathcal R` be the union of all closed strict-rhombus faces. For every physical rhombus-boundary edge occurrence bordering a bounded complementary component of `mathcal R`, the existing hole-charge theorem maps that occurrence injectively to the incidence of the same contact edge with the bounded exceptional face on its other side. Denote the image set of incidences by `I_hole`. Thus

\[
|I_{\rm hole}|=P_{\rm hole}.
\tag{SH.8}
\]

Now let `E_E` be the set of singleton stopped trains having at least one terminal incidence on a bounded exceptional face. By definition `|E_E|=S_E`. For each singleton edge choose one such bounded exceptional-face terminal incidence. Distinct singleton stopped trains are distinct contact edges, hence the chosen incidences form a set `I_sing` with

\[
|I_{\rm sing}|=S_E.
\tag{SH.9}
\]

The two incidence sets are disjoint. An incidence in `I_hole` belongs to an edge incident with a strict rhombus on its opposite side, so that edge lies in the strict-rhombus complex and belongs to a non-singleton rhombic stopped train. An incidence in `I_sing` belongs to a singleton stopped-train edge, which by the exact decomposition `T=T_R+S` lies in no strict rhombus. One contact edge cannot satisfy both descriptions.

Every incidence in either set is one of the bounded exceptional-face boundary incidences, whose total number is exactly

\[
\sum_F\deg F=4f+Q.
\tag{SH.10}
\]

Therefore

\[
P_{\rm hole}+S_E
=|I_{\rm hole}|+|I_{\rm sing}|
\le4f+Q,
\]

proving (SH.1).

No assumption is made that a singleton edge has only one exceptional side. If it has two, choosing either one is enough; the argument uses one distinct incidence per singleton train.

---

## 2. Hole and train-flex consequences

Every bounded complementary component of `mathcal R` has physical boundary length at least five in the triangle-free branch. Hence

\[
5g_R\le P_{\rm hole}.
\]

Together with (SH.1),

\[
5g_R+S_E\le4f+Q,
\]

which proves (SH.2).

The train-angle period theorem gives

\[
\dim\mathcal F_{\rm train}\ge T_R-2g_R.
\tag{SH.11}
\]

Using (SH.2),

\[
g_R\le{4f+Q-S_E\over5},
\]

so

\[
\dim\mathcal F_{\rm train}
\ge T_R-{2\over5}(4f+Q-S_E),
\]

proving (SH.3).

Likewise every rhombic train that is not unbounded-boundary-to-unbounded-boundary has at least one endpoint occurrence on a bounded hole. Assign one such occurrence to each such train. Distinct trains have distinct terminal boundary occurrences, so

\[
T_R-N_{oo}\le P_{\rm hole}.
\]

Equation (SH.1) gives

\[
N_{oo}\ge T_R-(4f+Q)+S_E,
\]

which is (SH.4).

The `N_oo` in this note is the rhombic-complex notion: both ends lie on the boundary of the unbounded complementary component of `mathcal R`. It is not identified with the narrower full-contact-graph notion of a train terminating twice on the actual outer contact face.

---

## 3. Composition with the detour-free gate

Write `Q=f+X`. From (SH.3),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\dim\mathcal F_{\rm train}
&\ge
2\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}
-2f-{2X\over5}+{2S_E\over5}\\
&=
2\sqrt{R_4}-2f-{2X\over5}
+{3\sigma_R\over10}
+{\sigma_R+2S_E\over5}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{SH.12}
\]

Insert (SH.5) to obtain (SH.6).

Similarly (SH.4) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_{oo}
&\ge
2\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}-5f-X+S_E\\
&=
2\sqrt{R_4}-5f-X
+{\sigma_R+2S_E\over2}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{SH.13}
\]

Using (SH.5) gives (SH.7).

The signs are important: the positive `X/2` supplied by (SH.5) cancels half of the `-X` exceptional-incidence cost in (SH.13). In the train-flex inequality it cancels one of the two fifths of `X`.

---

## 4. Endpoint-scale diagnostic

For orientation only, on the narrow formal profile

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\to\Delta_2,
\qquad
{f\over D}\to f_*,
\qquad
{X\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{k\over D}\to0,
\]

where

\[
f_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00021,
\]

(SH.7) gives

\[
\liminf {N_{oo}\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+{c_{DF}\over2}-5f_*.
\tag{SH.14}
\]

This coefficient is positive and of order one. The statement is only a diagnostic of the correlated ledger; the formal profile is not asserted for the surviving branch, and `N_oo` still concerns isolated-rhombus-complex shears rather than full-source feasible motions.

---

## 5. Scope audit and remaining implication

- `S_E` is counted once per singleton stopped train, not once per exceptional incidence.
- The proof chooses one exceptional incidence per `S_E` train; a train touching two exceptional faces is not double-counted.
- Hole incidences and chosen singleton incidences are disjoint because the former edges belong to at least one strict rhombus and the latter belong to none.
- Bridges, articulation vertices, repeated outer-face vertices, and contact-isolated source points are retained.
- (SH.3) is first-order. (SH.4) gives exact finite shears only in the isolated strict-rhombus subframework, exactly as in the parent theorem.
- No rank claim for external contact/diameter rows is strengthened here; the existing pure-row-count barrier remains valid.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or same-cardinality strict sub-`D` source is produced.

The next useful target is geometric locking: show that a linear subfamily of the `N_oo` coordinate shears cannot all be independently locked by the actual exceptional-face and diameter rows once the same exceptional incidences are already consumed by `S_E`, or combine the reduced hole budget with the global pentagon/two-window theorem to force a large signed-monotone train subsystem.

C-II and Level I remain open.
